WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.470 --> 00:00:02.980
- Jakob Nielsen recently
shared his prediction

00:00:02.980 --> 00:00:05.870
for what UX looks like in 2050.

00:00:05.870 --> 00:00:07.230
Here's his keynote speech

00:00:07.230 --> 00:00:10.340
from our virtual conference in 2020.

00:00:10.340 --> 00:00:11.600
- Well, thank you very much, Sarah.

00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:14.930
And thank you everybody for coming,

00:00:14.930 --> 00:00:16.770
joining us in particular,
of course, if it's

00:00:16.770 --> 00:00:19.270
in the middle of the night where you are.

00:00:19.270 --> 00:00:21.530
Since this is a February
conference, I thought I would start

00:00:21.530 --> 00:00:24.820
by just briefly talk about Numa Pompilius.

00:00:24.820 --> 00:00:27.920
And he lived 2,700 years ago.

00:00:27.920 --> 00:00:29.700
And Numa Pompilius is responsible

00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:32.290
for the months of January and February

00:00:32.290 --> 00:00:34.310
because before him the year

00:00:34.310 --> 00:00:36.940
only had 10 months in the calendar.

00:00:36.940 --> 00:00:38.850
And that's actually why the months

00:00:38.850 --> 00:00:42.160
of September, October,
November and December

00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:45.620
are named after number seven,
eight, nine, and 10 in Latin

00:00:45.620 --> 00:00:49.120
because those were the numbers
of those months at the time.

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:51.480
And then Numa Pompilius
introduced two more months

00:00:51.480 --> 00:00:53.970
and not at the end, put
them at the beginning.

00:00:53.970 --> 00:00:55.550
So the numbering scheme was messed up

00:00:55.550 --> 00:00:58.430
and also said that it was February

00:00:58.430 --> 00:01:01.940
which I think is a usability
abomination of a month.

00:01:01.940 --> 00:01:03.450
So that has caused so much trouble

00:01:03.450 --> 00:01:05.030
for all the little children

00:01:05.030 --> 00:01:07.490
for 2,700 years in learning the calendar

00:01:07.490 --> 00:01:09.350
so we can blame him for that.

00:01:09.350 --> 00:01:10.470
I just mentioned this story

00:01:10.470 --> 00:01:12.850
because it shows you how design decisions

00:01:12.850 --> 00:01:15.800
can sometimes last for a very long time

00:01:15.800 --> 00:01:17.560
though I don't think most
of the designs we work

00:01:17.560 --> 00:01:20.280
on will still be here in 2,700 years.

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:23.550
But even so that's why it
makes sense to look ahead

00:01:23.550 --> 00:01:24.740
and talk about sort of a little bit

00:01:24.740 --> 00:01:26.800
of a longer term picture.

00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:28.460
And they're really two different ways

00:01:28.460 --> 00:01:30.300
we can consider problems.

00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:33.180
And so by way of example,
I'm showing you two planets.

00:01:33.180 --> 00:01:36.600
We can look at planets,
study them bottom up

00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:37.630
like earth was studied.

00:01:37.630 --> 00:01:39.940
Like first you get hold of like one rock

00:01:39.940 --> 00:01:41.660
and you can kind of how's this rock.

00:01:41.660 --> 00:01:43.920
And you then you maybe
look at one volcano,

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:45.930
one Island to build up to a bigger,

00:01:45.930 --> 00:01:48.260
bigger understanding of that planet

00:01:48.260 --> 00:01:50.030
until you get to things
like continental drift

00:01:50.030 --> 00:01:51.230
and what have you.

00:01:51.230 --> 00:01:54.690
On the other hand, Mars
was studied a top-down.

00:01:54.690 --> 00:01:56.130
And so in the beginning, people just knew

00:01:56.130 --> 00:01:59.521
it was a red planet and we
still actually don't have rocks

00:01:59.521 --> 00:02:01.850
from Mars like under the microscope

00:02:01.850 --> 00:02:04.020
in the laboratories here,
there are some missions

00:02:04.020 --> 00:02:06.140
up to try to collect them but so far, no.

00:02:06.140 --> 00:02:08.060
Today we're gonna take
that kind of Mars view

00:02:08.060 --> 00:02:12.270
that a big global view, not
look at the individual rocks

00:02:12.270 --> 00:02:15.430
or individual volcanoes,
individual screen designs

00:02:15.430 --> 00:02:16.520
and buttons and what have you.

00:02:16.520 --> 00:02:17.690
We're gonna look at sort of a bigger,

00:02:17.690 --> 00:02:19.090
bigger picture scheme here.

00:02:19.090 --> 00:02:21.280
First, I always like to,
when try to look ahead,

00:02:21.280 --> 00:02:24.470
try this mirror to flip it
and look back the same amount

00:02:24.470 --> 00:02:27.190
of years to get us an
idea of what can happen

00:02:27.190 --> 00:02:29.040
at this timeframe we're looking at.

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:33.301
So 29 years ago, this screen
I'm showing here was an example

00:02:33.301 --> 00:02:37.370
of a type of interface
that we had at the time.

00:02:37.370 --> 00:02:41.910
So we had a multimedia, we
had hypertext but on CD-ROMs.

00:02:41.910 --> 00:02:45.616
The web had just barely been
invented the year before

00:02:45.616 --> 00:02:47.210
but it was very primitive.

00:02:47.210 --> 00:02:50.000
I actually saw the web demonstrated

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:53.120
at a hypertext conference in 1991

00:02:53.120 --> 00:02:55.230
and I was not very impressed.

00:02:55.230 --> 00:02:56.550
The web was...

00:02:56.550 --> 00:02:57.810
There was like five websites

00:02:57.810 --> 00:02:59.730
or something like that in the world.

00:02:59.730 --> 00:03:03.080
The next year when the graphical browser

00:03:03.080 --> 00:03:06.940
was introduced widely spread
that the web really took off.

00:03:06.940 --> 00:03:08.513
And also like in terms
of like, for example

00:03:08.513 --> 00:03:11.240
what were the famous computer
companies at the time.

00:03:11.240 --> 00:03:12.920
Well, some of them are still famous

00:03:12.920 --> 00:03:14.710
and Microsoft, IBM, Apple

00:03:14.710 --> 00:03:17.630
others don't even know exist anymore.

00:03:17.630 --> 00:03:20.580
Mobile, we had mobile
devices know as flip phones,

00:03:20.580 --> 00:03:22.040
didn't have smartphones.

00:03:22.040 --> 00:03:25.233
The data services were
completely, extremely primitive.

00:03:26.130 --> 00:03:29.260
So in terms of what's
kind of important now,

00:03:29.260 --> 00:03:31.520
there were just some fairly small kind

00:03:31.520 --> 00:03:34.090
of starting elements of that at the time

00:03:35.150 --> 00:03:37.360
but it's has been very
different of course.

00:03:37.360 --> 00:03:41.040
But that means that if
we look ahead to 2050,

00:03:41.040 --> 00:03:44.010
we can maybe expect that we
can sort of see some trends

00:03:44.010 --> 00:03:46.610
and that they may have some impact

00:03:46.610 --> 00:03:48.200
on what's gonna happen,

00:03:48.200 --> 00:03:50.680
but that said things are gonna
be very different as well.

00:03:50.680 --> 00:03:54.410
So that's why it makes sense
to look that much ahead.

00:03:54.410 --> 00:03:56.070
So here I have a list of 10

00:03:56.070 --> 00:03:59.760
of the most prominent
technology companies right now.

00:03:59.760 --> 00:04:02.570
And I do say 10 of,
because of course there's

00:04:02.570 --> 00:04:04.933
no official list of the 10
most important companies

00:04:04.933 --> 00:04:06.930
or anything like that.

00:04:06.930 --> 00:04:09.690
But they are for sure
10 of the most important

00:04:09.690 --> 00:04:11.720
and that's the most important

00:04:11.720 --> 00:04:12.950
and interesting thing about this list

00:04:12.950 --> 00:04:15.520
is companies marked with star

00:04:15.520 --> 00:04:16.470
because those are the ones

00:04:16.470 --> 00:04:19.090
that actually existed back in 1990.

00:04:19.090 --> 00:04:22.340
So most of them are new and similarly

00:04:22.340 --> 00:04:24.130
if we look ahead to 2050,

00:04:24.130 --> 00:04:26.480
we would expect if we
deal to a similar list

00:04:26.480 --> 00:04:30.700
then of what are the most
important technology companies,

00:04:30.700 --> 00:04:32.450
most likely, most of them
are gonna be companies

00:04:32.450 --> 00:04:34.000
that don't exist now.

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:35.750
And so new things are gonna happen.

00:04:36.736 --> 00:04:38.780
And one of the big things
that I think is definitely

00:04:38.780 --> 00:04:41.330
gonna happen is we think about 2050

00:04:41.330 --> 00:04:43.453
the economy of China is
expected to be about two

00:04:43.453 --> 00:04:47.380
and a half times as big as the
economy of the United States.

00:04:47.380 --> 00:04:50.400
So we should expect a lot
more Chinese companies

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:52.400
on this type of list.

00:04:52.400 --> 00:04:53.980
Also, maybe even more importantly

00:04:53.980 --> 00:04:57.310
the economy of all of Asia put together

00:04:57.310 --> 00:05:00.340
will be about three times
the economy of North America

00:05:00.340 --> 00:05:01.610
and Europe put together.

00:05:01.610 --> 00:05:03.740
So we should also expect companies

00:05:03.740 --> 00:05:07.330
from other places in Asia
and not just from China.

00:05:07.330 --> 00:05:09.230
But of course exactly which countries

00:05:09.230 --> 00:05:10.790
and exactly which companies

00:05:10.790 --> 00:05:13.840
and what they're gonna be
doing, that is very hard to say.

00:05:13.840 --> 00:05:16.620
But the main point I
think that's important

00:05:16.620 --> 00:05:18.780
that we can recognize already now

00:05:18.780 --> 00:05:21.510
is dramatic much more
internationalization,

00:05:21.510 --> 00:05:25.050
globalization, broadening
of where technology

00:05:25.050 --> 00:05:26.730
and innovation is gonna come from

00:05:26.730 --> 00:05:27.790
compared to how it's been in the past

00:05:27.790 --> 00:05:31.543
where it was very kind of
isolated and monopolized.

00:05:32.570 --> 00:05:35.090
This chart shows the growth

00:05:35.090 --> 00:05:37.583
in gross national product per person.

00:05:38.900 --> 00:05:40.020
And I'm showing you this

00:05:40.020 --> 00:05:43.710
because this is the single
most important parameter

00:05:43.710 --> 00:05:47.040
for human welfare and
our standard of living.

00:05:47.040 --> 00:05:48.670
It's of course not the only one

00:05:48.670 --> 00:05:51.940
but it's extremely highly
correlated with almost

00:05:51.940 --> 00:05:54.350
all other things you might wanna measure,

00:05:54.350 --> 00:05:56.160
like percentage of households

00:05:56.160 --> 00:05:58.760
that have indoor plumbing
or air condition,

00:05:58.760 --> 00:06:01.710
infant mortality, amount of pollution.

00:06:01.710 --> 00:06:03.220
I mean, whatever you wanna measure

00:06:03.220 --> 00:06:05.320
it's usually very strongly
correlated with this.

00:06:05.320 --> 00:06:07.773
So it's a single measure
that's nice to have.

00:06:09.570 --> 00:06:11.770
And you can see that it's basically

00:06:11.770 --> 00:06:14.690
this chart only goes back to the 1700,

00:06:14.690 --> 00:06:17.970
but we can draw it back all
the way back to year one,

00:06:17.970 --> 00:06:19.470
the age of the Roman empire.

00:06:19.470 --> 00:06:22.430
And it was just a piece of flat
line all the way going back

00:06:22.430 --> 00:06:26.080
because that's how it's been
for all of human history.

00:06:26.080 --> 00:06:26.930
Two things really.

00:06:26.930 --> 00:06:29.100
First of all, no change.

00:06:29.100 --> 00:06:31.810
One decade was the same
as the other decade.

00:06:31.810 --> 00:06:34.280
I mean, gradually no change

00:06:34.280 --> 00:06:37.550
but no change that you
could actually feel.

00:06:37.550 --> 00:06:39.420
Of course in the country
the reason it makes sense

00:06:39.420 --> 00:06:41.940
to talk about 2050 is we
expect there will be change.

00:06:41.940 --> 00:06:43.800
So that's our current such scenario

00:06:43.800 --> 00:06:45.900
but that did not use to be the case.

00:06:45.900 --> 00:06:48.940
Also, it used to be the case
that everybody was dirt poor.

00:06:48.940 --> 00:06:52.140
The line was like very,
very, very close to zero.

00:06:52.140 --> 00:06:53.810
And of course it's not literally true.

00:06:53.810 --> 00:06:56.240
There were a few members of
the nobility who were rich

00:06:56.240 --> 00:06:59.140
and of course the king was very rich.

00:06:59.140 --> 00:07:00.612
You have to remember though

00:07:00.612 --> 00:07:05.180
even the king didn't get
good wifi in the palace.

00:07:05.180 --> 00:07:09.720
So, but we have had this
great improvement in welfare.

00:07:09.720 --> 00:07:11.290
And just to give you one example

00:07:11.290 --> 00:07:13.330
of how the concrete of this,

00:07:13.330 --> 00:07:15.600
I'm looking at it as a bag of flour.

00:07:15.600 --> 00:07:17.480
This is one of the very few products

00:07:17.480 --> 00:07:20.600
that actually been the same for centuries.

00:07:20.600 --> 00:07:23.850
And with the benefit of utility
for a person is the same.

00:07:23.850 --> 00:07:25.800
You can bake the same amount of bread now

00:07:25.800 --> 00:07:27.000
as you could back then

00:07:27.000 --> 00:07:28.847
and you can eat the
same amount of bread now

00:07:28.847 --> 00:07:30.250
as you could back then.

00:07:30.250 --> 00:07:31.950
So it's the same benefit.

00:07:31.950 --> 00:07:35.460
But back in 1500, an
average worker had to work

00:07:35.460 --> 00:07:38.690
for three days to make enough money to buy

00:07:38.690 --> 00:07:40.250
this kind of bag of flour.

00:07:40.250 --> 00:07:42.220
And today an average worker,

00:07:42.220 --> 00:07:44.190
average being half make
more-half make less

00:07:44.190 --> 00:07:47.570
an average worker can buy 89 bags of flour

00:07:47.570 --> 00:07:49.400
for one day's salary.

00:07:49.400 --> 00:07:51.270
So you combine those two and you can see

00:07:51.270 --> 00:07:58.083
that we are 266 times better
off for the average worker.

00:07:58.083 --> 00:07:59.790
And this doesn't even take into account

00:07:59.790 --> 00:08:03.700
that a day's work in year
1500 was much harder.

00:08:03.700 --> 00:08:07.610
I mean, being a farmhand
in 1500 was very hard work

00:08:07.610 --> 00:08:09.930
compared to the jobs people have today.

00:08:09.930 --> 00:08:13.790
So total, this is improvement
of about 1.1% per year.

00:08:13.790 --> 00:08:15.210
So very little.

00:08:15.210 --> 00:08:17.450
And actually that's probably comprised

00:08:17.450 --> 00:08:20.630
of about 0.1% improvement per year

00:08:20.630 --> 00:08:21.980
for the first three centuries

00:08:21.980 --> 00:08:24.080
because again, things
didn't change very much.

00:08:24.080 --> 00:08:25.500
And then the last two centuries

00:08:25.500 --> 00:08:27.430
may be more like 2% per year.

00:08:27.430 --> 00:08:29.630
Actually just small percentages every year

00:08:29.630 --> 00:08:32.550
they add up to huge, huge, huge advances.

00:08:32.550 --> 00:08:34.087
And it's not it's always only goes up.

00:08:34.087 --> 00:08:37.180
I mean you can see here,
here are kind of the dips

00:08:37.180 --> 00:08:39.960
for the recession that we're in right now.

00:08:39.960 --> 00:08:42.650
So there's certainly things
can get worse, of course.

00:08:42.650 --> 00:08:44.730
But basically they have
been getting better

00:08:44.730 --> 00:08:48.180
and it varies between
countries when that started.

00:08:48.180 --> 00:08:51.630
In Europe, United States, it
started around the year 1800

00:08:51.630 --> 00:08:53.940
things started really
changing and getting better.

00:08:53.940 --> 00:08:57.720
In Singapore, not until
around the year 1960,

00:08:57.720 --> 00:09:00.400
in China not even until the year 1980

00:09:00.400 --> 00:09:02.670
did they really start and
that kind of improvement curve

00:09:02.670 --> 00:09:04.930
of things constantly getting
better and better and better.

00:09:04.930 --> 00:09:06.440
And why is it getting better?

00:09:06.440 --> 00:09:08.140
'Cause it doesn't happen by itself.

00:09:08.140 --> 00:09:10.610
The reason we are so well off today,

00:09:10.610 --> 00:09:12.170
it's really to the credit of probably

00:09:12.170 --> 00:09:14.240
at least a 1 million different people.

00:09:14.240 --> 00:09:17.520
Individuals like engineers
inventing a new product

00:09:17.520 --> 00:09:20.590
or business people inventing a new way

00:09:20.590 --> 00:09:22.310
of meeting some new customer needs

00:09:22.310 --> 00:09:24.380
or running their company more efficiently

00:09:24.380 --> 00:09:25.820
so people could afford the products

00:09:25.820 --> 00:09:28.770
that engineers invented and so forth.

00:09:28.770 --> 00:09:30.190
And so some of these million people

00:09:30.190 --> 00:09:31.610
are certainly more famous than others,

00:09:31.610 --> 00:09:33.330
but most of them we don't know.

00:09:33.330 --> 00:09:35.960
Well, I know Thomas Edison
got a thousand patents,

00:09:35.960 --> 00:09:37.440
very famous inventor.

00:09:37.440 --> 00:09:40.600
Or Henry Ford, very famous business person

00:09:40.600 --> 00:09:43.170
came up with a way of running
his company so efficiently

00:09:43.170 --> 00:09:46.110
that normal people could
afford to buy a car

00:09:46.110 --> 00:09:48.440
and before that it was
just for rich people.

00:09:48.440 --> 00:09:50.970
So those are great individual improvements

00:09:50.970 --> 00:09:52.700
but again it's really the majority

00:09:52.700 --> 00:09:54.480
of the much smaller improvements

00:09:54.480 --> 00:09:57.240
that just add up and add up and add up.

00:09:57.240 --> 00:09:58.610
Because again, I mean, nobody uses

00:09:58.610 --> 00:10:01.160
a Thomas Edison light bulb today, right?

00:10:01.160 --> 00:10:05.000
We have these led light bulbs
that are like much brighter,

00:10:05.000 --> 00:10:06.830
use much less electricity

00:10:06.830 --> 00:10:10.541
and they run for 10
years without breaking.

00:10:10.541 --> 00:10:12.950
And this is not like one
person invented that light bulb

00:10:12.950 --> 00:10:14.840
and we threw out the old light bulbs

00:10:14.840 --> 00:10:16.290
from Thomas Edison, right?

00:10:16.290 --> 00:10:20.020
A lot of smaller improvements
over the years since Edison.

00:10:20.020 --> 00:10:24.140
And so this is what we also
would expect going forward.

00:10:24.140 --> 00:10:27.230
But there's an interesting
kind of difference I think.

00:10:27.230 --> 00:10:31.100
And so this chart is measured in money

00:10:31.100 --> 00:10:34.050
but money is just a
measurement unit for resources

00:10:34.050 --> 00:10:37.150
and so it means how much can we do.

00:10:37.150 --> 00:10:39.710
So we're gonna get much
richer than we are now.

00:10:39.710 --> 00:10:41.810
We are already very rich
compared to our ancestors

00:10:41.810 --> 00:10:43.240
but we will get even more rich.

00:10:43.240 --> 00:10:46.163
And so when people get
very rich, what happens is

00:10:46.163 --> 00:10:49.330
that they will demand quality

00:10:49.330 --> 00:10:52.440
and UX is a quality discipline.

00:10:52.440 --> 00:10:56.040
We are the ones who are
gonna make these products

00:10:56.040 --> 00:10:59.603
and services better to
satisfy these rich people

00:10:59.603 --> 00:11:01.890
that we all gonna be in the future.

00:11:01.890 --> 00:11:03.540
That's our job, our role.

00:11:03.540 --> 00:11:05.850
And this is why there'll
be much more demand for us

00:11:05.850 --> 00:11:10.600
in the future is because people
will be able to afford it.

00:11:10.600 --> 00:11:12.830
I mean, they will want
better, better, and better

00:11:12.830 --> 00:11:15.020
and even better things all the time

00:11:15.020 --> 00:11:17.640
because they're getting
richer and richer and richer.

00:11:17.640 --> 00:11:19.790
And another thing is also gonna happen

00:11:19.790 --> 00:11:22.550
is that again, these curves
don't go up just by themselves.

00:11:22.550 --> 00:11:25.820
They go up because of
improvements, innovation

00:11:25.820 --> 00:11:29.700
and they will for sure still
be innovation in other fields

00:11:29.700 --> 00:11:33.400
like agriculture, like a bag
of flour will be even cheaper

00:11:33.400 --> 00:11:38.400
by 2050, manufacturing industry
will be improved as well

00:11:38.400 --> 00:11:39.670
for sure, for sure.

00:11:39.670 --> 00:11:43.737
But a lot of these additional
enhancements to the economy

00:11:43.737 --> 00:11:45.510
are gonna come from us

00:11:45.510 --> 00:11:48.910
because now we are in a
knowledge economy mainly,

00:11:48.910 --> 00:11:50.200
we also have agriculture,

00:11:50.200 --> 00:11:51.800
we also have the other things, right?

00:11:51.800 --> 00:11:55.350
But we are a lot to a great
extent in a knowledge economy.

00:11:55.350 --> 00:11:59.790
And to improve knowledge work
that is user experience work

00:11:59.790 --> 00:12:03.780
to make people more effective
and efficient and innovative

00:12:03.780 --> 00:12:07.590
and just all these good
things, that is our job.

00:12:07.590 --> 00:12:11.230
So I think both from the demand
side and the supply side,

00:12:11.230 --> 00:12:16.571
we are going to be a very,
very key part of this

00:12:16.571 --> 00:12:18.960
economic growth that's gonna happen

00:12:18.960 --> 00:12:21.280
over the next 30 years or so.

00:12:21.280 --> 00:12:25.981
And so that's why I believe
that there will be an enormous,

00:12:25.981 --> 00:12:28.210
huge, more use of UX.

00:12:28.210 --> 00:12:29.700
Many, many, many more UX people

00:12:29.700 --> 00:12:31.770
which we'll return to in a minute.

00:12:31.770 --> 00:12:34.210
But first let's talk
about the broadening of UX

00:12:34.210 --> 00:12:36.090
in terms of internationalization.

00:12:36.090 --> 00:12:38.820
So this chart here
shows you the percentage

00:12:38.820 --> 00:12:41.480
of the world's population
that's on the internet

00:12:41.480 --> 00:12:44.890
and over only a 15-year period,
which is pretty short time,

00:12:44.890 --> 00:12:47.830
it's gone up by a factor of three.

00:12:47.830 --> 00:12:49.480
And so of course we can not go up

00:12:49.480 --> 00:12:50.880
by a factor of three again

00:12:50.880 --> 00:12:52.197
when we are already at
more than half, right?

00:12:52.197 --> 00:12:53.680
But we can almost double

00:12:53.680 --> 00:12:55.480
and that's what I would
predict will happen

00:12:55.480 --> 00:12:56.590
over the next 30 years.

00:12:56.590 --> 00:12:59.010
And I would predict that by 2050,

00:12:59.010 --> 00:13:01.230
we'll never get to a 100% know exactly,

00:13:01.230 --> 00:13:02.540
there's always gonna be a few people

00:13:02.540 --> 00:13:04.120
who don't wanna go online.

00:13:04.120 --> 00:13:05.530
But I believe we'll get to the point

00:13:05.530 --> 00:13:08.220
where basically everybody
all over the world

00:13:08.220 --> 00:13:10.440
in all countries are on the internet

00:13:10.440 --> 00:13:11.880
and that means we have to design for them.

00:13:11.880 --> 00:13:14.890
And again, this is gonna
be much, much broadening

00:13:14.890 --> 00:13:17.003
of the use of base compared
to what we have now

00:13:17.003 --> 00:13:20.780
and particularly what we
had those 15 years ago.

00:13:20.780 --> 00:13:23.050
We can even see this just
at our own conference,

00:13:23.050 --> 00:13:26.150
the UX Conference, that we have people now

00:13:26.150 --> 00:13:27.830
coming from so many more countries

00:13:27.830 --> 00:13:29.650
and territories than we're used to

00:13:29.650 --> 00:13:32.070
even this just small amount of time

00:13:32.070 --> 00:13:33.740
by which I compiled the data.

00:13:33.740 --> 00:13:38.070
In 2015, we had people
from 69 different countries

00:13:38.070 --> 00:13:40.410
and last year we had
85 different countries.

00:13:40.410 --> 00:13:41.820
So it goes up, up, up,

00:13:41.820 --> 00:13:45.460
broader, broader, broader as spread of UX

00:13:45.460 --> 00:13:46.660
which I think is very happy.

00:13:46.660 --> 00:13:49.620
But it's one of those big, big trends

00:13:49.620 --> 00:13:52.330
that we are getting to be a much broader

00:13:52.330 --> 00:13:57.600
and much bigger field
than we were in the past.

00:13:57.600 --> 00:14:00.740
So another prediction that's
very safe is the distribution

00:14:00.740 --> 00:14:03.020
of the population by 2050

00:14:03.020 --> 00:14:04.200
because most of the people

00:14:04.200 --> 00:14:07.420
who will be alive then are already alive.

00:14:07.420 --> 00:14:09.690
And so we know how many
old people they will be

00:14:09.690 --> 00:14:11.820
because they are the
middle aged people now.

00:14:11.820 --> 00:14:13.470
Now we don't know exactly
how many young people

00:14:13.470 --> 00:14:15.960
there will be because they
have not been born yet.

00:14:15.960 --> 00:14:17.640
But the people who work on specialized

00:14:17.640 --> 00:14:18.740
in starting these things,

00:14:18.740 --> 00:14:20.860
they have some pretty
decent projections of it

00:14:20.860 --> 00:14:22.950
and that's what I'm showing you here.

00:14:22.950 --> 00:14:25.020
And it varies between countries

00:14:25.020 --> 00:14:27.400
but the broad conclusion
is really the same

00:14:27.400 --> 00:14:30.370
which is that will be more
old people than young people.

00:14:30.370 --> 00:14:34.540
And that means that we have
to design more for old people

00:14:34.540 --> 00:14:36.030
and we don't do that.

00:14:36.030 --> 00:14:37.900
Well, now that's a big disgrace

00:14:37.900 --> 00:14:39.070
in the user experience field.

00:14:39.070 --> 00:14:42.560
I have to say how we are
ignoring elderly users.

00:14:42.560 --> 00:14:46.180
And it's even more so if you
look at split this up now

00:14:46.180 --> 00:14:50.110
in not just old people, but
young-old people and old,-old.

00:14:50.110 --> 00:14:52.270
And so the young old you can see here

00:14:52.270 --> 00:14:54.390
that's gonna grow
somewhat in most countries

00:14:54.390 --> 00:14:55.420
except in Japan.

00:14:55.420 --> 00:14:57.590
Actually it's about the same

00:14:57.590 --> 00:14:59.680
because they already
have a lot of old people.

00:14:59.680 --> 00:15:04.370
But the old-old people
are gonna be hugely more

00:15:04.370 --> 00:15:06.070
in all countries.

00:15:06.070 --> 00:15:08.490
And from a UX perspective,
that is important

00:15:08.490 --> 00:15:11.640
because the young-old people
are actually not that different

00:15:11.640 --> 00:15:14.890
than middle aged people in
terms of what design they need.

00:15:14.890 --> 00:15:18.360
But for the old-old, 85
years and older people,

00:15:18.360 --> 00:15:21.840
we had to really do have to
take a lot of special things

00:15:21.840 --> 00:15:23.880
into account when designing for them.

00:15:23.880 --> 00:15:26.750
And we don't do that very well now

00:15:26.750 --> 00:15:29.140
and that's one of the things
we really do have to change

00:15:29.140 --> 00:15:30.240
in the upcoming years

00:15:30.240 --> 00:15:33.380
because of the user population changing.

00:15:33.380 --> 00:15:35.550
So we have to change the
design for the actual people.

00:15:35.550 --> 00:15:38.550
That's one of our main principles.

00:15:38.550 --> 00:15:41.840
So I'm predicting a broad note increase

00:15:41.840 --> 00:15:44.470
in user experience workforce.

00:15:44.470 --> 00:15:46.380
And I'm gonna take a kind of broad view,

00:15:46.380 --> 00:15:49.630
a 100-year view actually
of this going back to 1950

00:15:49.630 --> 00:15:53.170
when the field started and
going forward to the 2050

00:15:53.170 --> 00:15:56.050
that's our kind of target
date we're looking at here.

00:15:56.050 --> 00:15:59.520
And I'm gonna look at the different levels

00:15:59.520 --> 00:16:02.700
of intelligence and IQ
intelligence quotient

00:16:02.700 --> 00:16:04.610
that's needed to do good UX work.

00:16:04.610 --> 00:16:06.290
Because I think that has
actually been changing

00:16:06.290 --> 00:16:08.930
over the years which is
an interesting point.

00:16:08.930 --> 00:16:11.530
And before I do that, I'll
just have to first acknowledge

00:16:11.530 --> 00:16:14.268
that intelligence is not the only factor

00:16:14.268 --> 00:16:16.570
for how good people
are at user experience,

00:16:16.570 --> 00:16:19.430
there are many other things
that impact that too.

00:16:19.430 --> 00:16:21.940
But I'm talking about
IQ here for two reasons.

00:16:21.940 --> 00:16:26.130
The first is that it is the
number one most important number

00:16:26.130 --> 00:16:28.510
for any knowledge work
including user experience.

00:16:28.510 --> 00:16:30.620
But for all knowledge
work, how smart you are

00:16:30.620 --> 00:16:33.360
is the number one influence.

00:16:33.360 --> 00:16:36.900
And secondly, intelligence
has been studied very,

00:16:36.900 --> 00:16:39.300
very detailed for about 100 years.

00:16:39.300 --> 00:16:42.230
And so we have very, very
detailed knowledge about

00:16:42.230 --> 00:16:44.220
how many people exist in the world

00:16:44.220 --> 00:16:46.290
at different levels of intelligence.

00:16:46.290 --> 00:16:47.123
But before I got into that,

00:16:47.123 --> 00:16:48.970
let me just kind of
acknowledged this point

00:16:48.970 --> 00:16:51.370
that intelligence is not the only thing.

00:16:51.370 --> 00:16:53.520
And so, as a little example,

00:16:53.520 --> 00:16:56.900
I'm here looking at
three different UX people

00:16:56.900 --> 00:16:59.610
so person one, person
two and persons three.

00:16:59.610 --> 00:17:01.980
And each dot is one thing that

00:17:01.980 --> 00:17:04.360
that person has done in a week.

00:17:04.360 --> 00:17:07.290
And furthermore, we're
distributing those things according

00:17:07.290 --> 00:17:11.180
to their quality on a
scale that I just made up.

00:17:11.180 --> 00:17:14.210
A one to 10 scale for how
good the work product is.

00:17:14.210 --> 00:17:17.010
So work product, it could
be like a screen design,

00:17:17.010 --> 00:17:19.660
it could be a research
finding about users,

00:17:19.660 --> 00:17:24.070
it could be copywriting,
something content you wrote

00:17:24.070 --> 00:17:26.930
depending on what
subdiscipline of UX you're in.

00:17:26.930 --> 00:17:30.110
But just one thing the person has done.

00:17:30.110 --> 00:17:33.060
And it's definitely true
that things vary in quality

00:17:33.060 --> 00:17:34.470
and some things are better than others.

00:17:34.470 --> 00:17:36.200
And for any given person,

00:17:36.200 --> 00:17:38.210
sometimes they do better
than their average

00:17:38.210 --> 00:17:40.270
and sometimes they do
worse than their average.

00:17:40.270 --> 00:17:41.591
But we have the other three people

00:17:41.591 --> 00:17:42.966
and there are two different averages.

00:17:42.966 --> 00:17:46.560
Person one and two have
an average quality of 5.5

00:17:46.560 --> 00:17:48.240
and person three has a little bit

00:17:48.240 --> 00:17:50.200
lower quality unfortunately.

00:17:50.200 --> 00:17:52.700
And person one gets 22
things done in a week

00:17:52.700 --> 00:17:55.740
and person two and three get
44 things done in a week.

00:17:55.740 --> 00:17:56.573
Okay. Given this data,

00:17:56.573 --> 00:17:59.403
if you're gonna hire one
person for your UX team,

00:17:59.403 --> 00:18:02.113
who should that be? 
Person one, two or three?

00:18:03.102 --> 00:18:05.580
Well, okay.

00:18:05.580 --> 00:18:08.000
The answer here is very
obvious it's person two

00:18:08.000 --> 00:18:10.420
'cause they get a lot done
and it's high quality.

00:18:10.420 --> 00:18:12.573
Okay, let's say person
two is not available now

00:18:12.573 --> 00:18:14.390
it becomes interesting.

00:18:14.390 --> 00:18:15.790
So we have only the choice

00:18:15.790 --> 00:18:18.140
between person one and person three.

00:18:18.140 --> 00:18:21.562
Let me furthermore say
as an assumption here,

00:18:21.562 --> 00:18:25.250
we are in a company that
place very high value

00:18:25.250 --> 00:18:27.560
on high quality user experience.

00:18:27.560 --> 00:18:30.610
So that's our main goal is high quality.

00:18:30.610 --> 00:18:32.030
Should you now hire person one?

00:18:32.030 --> 00:18:33.547
They do less work, but it's high quality

00:18:33.547 --> 00:18:36.280
and person three they do
more work, but lower quality.

00:18:36.280 --> 00:18:37.870
And I want high quality.

00:18:37.870 --> 00:18:40.048
You might think that I
should take person one,

00:18:40.048 --> 00:18:43.330
but not so under one more assumption.

00:18:43.330 --> 00:18:46.980
And that assumption is we cannot
actually deliver everything

00:18:46.980 --> 00:18:48.800
that a UX team can come up with (laughs).

00:18:48.800 --> 00:18:51.090
And I think this is a
very realistic assumption

00:18:51.090 --> 00:18:54.710
because honestly designers
can design so many more things

00:18:54.710 --> 00:18:57.120
than the developers can
ever actually implement

00:18:57.120 --> 00:19:00.490
or the UX researchers can come
up with so many more insights

00:19:00.490 --> 00:19:03.290
about users than we can ever
actually design and deliver

00:19:03.290 --> 00:19:05.460
and implement and see up in the product.

00:19:05.460 --> 00:19:07.450
So for the sake of this argument

00:19:07.450 --> 00:19:10.100
I'm just saying the top 10
things we can actually do

00:19:10.100 --> 00:19:11.990
and the rest we cannot do.

00:19:11.990 --> 00:19:13.320
And US we're kind of lucky

00:19:13.320 --> 00:19:15.830
that we could probably actually
find the 10 best things

00:19:15.830 --> 00:19:18.634
because we have evaluation methods.

00:19:18.634 --> 00:19:22.030
So we know what's a good,
better design and other designs.

00:19:22.030 --> 00:19:24.470
So if we only do the top 10 things

00:19:24.470 --> 00:19:27.250
and now we compare person
one and person three,

00:19:27.250 --> 00:19:30.920
we see that the average quality
of the top 10 things only

00:19:30.920 --> 00:19:33.060
is actually higher for person three

00:19:33.060 --> 00:19:35.940
even though the overall
average quality is lower.

00:19:35.940 --> 00:19:38.240
And that's because they
have a very large number

00:19:38.240 --> 00:19:40.710
of things that will not gonna be done.

00:19:40.710 --> 00:19:41.543
And so what?

00:19:41.543 --> 00:19:43.270
It doesn't matter they're
lower quality, right?

00:19:43.270 --> 00:19:45.630
Because there's so many
more things to choose from,

00:19:45.630 --> 00:19:48.110
the quality of those few things we can do

00:19:48.110 --> 00:19:49.800
ends up being higher.

00:19:49.800 --> 00:19:51.460
So that's one of the points of sorts.

00:19:51.460 --> 00:19:54.370
It's not just talent like
hard work matters as well

00:19:54.370 --> 00:19:57.600
and many other criteria
as well matters as well.

00:19:57.600 --> 00:20:00.740
But let's go back to
just looking at the IQ.

00:20:00.740 --> 00:20:04.340
So back in 1950, it was
only a handful of people

00:20:04.340 --> 00:20:06.220
doing UX in the world.

00:20:06.220 --> 00:20:09.370
And it really started to build
laboratories or build apps

00:20:09.370 --> 00:20:12.500
which was the research laboratory

00:20:12.500 --> 00:20:15.230
for the telephone companies
in the United States.

00:20:15.230 --> 00:20:20.640
And build apps was a truly
highly, highly elite laboratory.

00:20:20.640 --> 00:20:23.260
It was probably the number
one research lab ever

00:20:23.260 --> 00:20:25.280
in the history of humanity.

00:20:25.280 --> 00:20:27.800
They invented things like
the transistor, the laser

00:20:27.800 --> 00:20:33.360
the radio telescope, mobile
cellular telephony, Unix

00:20:33.360 --> 00:20:34.440
whether you liked Unix

00:20:34.440 --> 00:20:37.260
that it was a major invention at the time,

00:20:37.260 --> 00:20:39.180
the touchstone of push-button telephone.

00:20:39.180 --> 00:20:41.970
All of these things many, many many more.

00:20:41.970 --> 00:20:45.510
Nobel prize winners walked
the hallways at this lab.

00:20:45.510 --> 00:20:47.730
It was really very, very super elite

00:20:47.730 --> 00:20:48.900
and they hired only people

00:20:48.900 --> 00:20:53.220
with these kind of pretty
close to genius level IQ.

00:20:53.220 --> 00:20:54.680
And so that was kinda lucky

00:20:54.680 --> 00:20:56.160
because I'll give you an example

00:20:56.160 --> 00:20:57.760
of one of the projects that they worked on

00:20:57.760 --> 00:20:59.800
which was in the touch tone telephone

00:20:59.800 --> 00:21:01.620
or the push button telephone.

00:21:01.620 --> 00:21:03.800
And here we see some of
the different layouts

00:21:03.800 --> 00:21:05.720
that they designed and they worked on

00:21:05.720 --> 00:21:07.530
and luckily they did the study.

00:21:07.530 --> 00:21:10.500
And so you can see that preferred is what

00:21:10.500 --> 00:21:13.990
show it to some customers,
"what telephone would you like?"

00:21:13.990 --> 00:21:15.840
But that was not the one that was the best

00:21:15.840 --> 00:21:18.360
and the fastest for
entering telephone numbers.

00:21:18.360 --> 00:21:20.710
That was actually the one
that's here's marked as fast.

00:21:20.710 --> 00:21:23.860
And that you can see this is
the same one we have today.

00:21:23.860 --> 00:21:28.050
And so now, if I tell
you you cannot choose

00:21:28.050 --> 00:21:30.610
the best design just
by showing some options

00:21:30.610 --> 00:21:33.480
to the customers and ask them,
"Which one would you like?"

00:21:33.480 --> 00:21:35.680
Honestly, we teach this
in day one now, right?

00:21:35.680 --> 00:21:37.970
Everybody who does user experience knows

00:21:37.970 --> 00:21:39.840
that you can't just show things

00:21:39.840 --> 00:21:41.860
to customers and ask them what they want,

00:21:41.860 --> 00:21:43.880
you have to do observational research,

00:21:43.880 --> 00:21:44.970
we all know this.

00:21:44.970 --> 00:21:49.000
But now let's backtrack
in time to the 1950s,

00:21:49.000 --> 00:21:50.510
they did not know that.

00:21:50.510 --> 00:21:54.960
This was the first set of
UX work for product design.

00:21:54.960 --> 00:21:56.527
And they didn't know anything of that,

00:21:56.527 --> 00:21:57.910
any of the things we know today.

00:21:57.910 --> 00:21:59.690
They had to discover all that

00:21:59.690 --> 00:22:03.892
and that's why they had to
be incredibly smart people,

00:22:03.892 --> 00:22:06.069
obviously just kind of
genius level research.

00:22:06.069 --> 00:22:07.720
And today we don't have to have that

00:22:07.720 --> 00:22:10.070
because we know we have
all these best practices.

00:22:10.070 --> 00:22:12.450
We know these things based on decades

00:22:12.450 --> 00:22:15.550
and decades of people already finding out.

00:22:15.550 --> 00:22:18.780
Another example from this early work.

00:22:18.780 --> 00:22:21.040
They also looked not only
at the layout of the buttons

00:22:21.040 --> 00:22:22.940
but also the buttons themselves.

00:22:22.940 --> 00:22:25.050
So it should be a big
button to small buttons.

00:22:25.050 --> 00:22:27.360
Well, they discovered that
big buttons you can type

00:22:27.360 --> 00:22:30.550
into the phone number faster
and you've make fewer errors.

00:22:30.550 --> 00:22:35.540
Now, do you need a big brain
to design a big button?

00:22:35.540 --> 00:22:36.660
Today, no.

00:22:36.660 --> 00:22:39.550
Today, everybody who works at
UX hopefully knows this rule

00:22:39.550 --> 00:22:41.580
that bigger buttons are faster to push.

00:22:41.580 --> 00:22:43.750
I mean fits as long all this...

00:22:43.750 --> 00:22:46.940
Again, something we teach
in the basic courses.

00:22:46.940 --> 00:22:49.960
And so all people do UX
should at least know this

00:22:49.960 --> 00:22:53.600
and should design as big buttons
as they can get away with.

00:22:53.600 --> 00:22:55.330
But back then, this was not know.

00:22:55.330 --> 00:22:57.340
How would you know that it
could be that small buttons

00:22:57.340 --> 00:22:59.840
were like you don't have to
move your finger so much, right?

00:22:59.840 --> 00:23:02.840
So nobody knows or
nobody knew I should say.

00:23:02.840 --> 00:23:04.327
And therefore they did...

00:23:04.327 --> 00:23:06.250
And they didn't know
how to study this right

00:23:06.250 --> 00:23:08.210
so you had to come up with all of that.

00:23:08.210 --> 00:23:10.036
And I'm very happy they did

00:23:10.036 --> 00:23:13.570
(indistinct) had these genius
people, UX people working

00:23:13.570 --> 00:23:15.550
on the push-button telephone

00:23:15.550 --> 00:23:17.460
because that design that they came up with

00:23:17.460 --> 00:23:20.810
that we are still using today,
has been used approximately

00:23:20.810 --> 00:23:26.540
40 trillion times, 40 trillion
times of usage of this design

00:23:26.540 --> 00:23:27.820
since it was launched.

00:23:27.820 --> 00:23:31.740
And this means that by
doing the better design

00:23:31.740 --> 00:23:36.210
they have saved the world
about 1 million person years.

00:23:36.210 --> 00:23:38.160
So those guys who did that study

00:23:38.160 --> 00:23:40.190
that was where we have really benefited,

00:23:40.190 --> 00:23:43.550
humanity's benefited a lot from that work.

00:23:43.550 --> 00:23:47.050
But, today we don't need to be that smart.

00:23:47.050 --> 00:23:49.193
We still definitely have to be
smart to do user experience,

00:23:49.193 --> 00:23:51.530
it's still difficult

00:23:51.530 --> 00:23:53.530
but we don't have to be geniuses anymore.

00:23:53.530 --> 00:23:55.690
And I predict when we go forward,

00:23:55.690 --> 00:23:57.120
we can actually lower

00:23:57.120 --> 00:23:59.790
the intelligence requirements even more

00:23:59.790 --> 00:24:03.370
because there'll be even more
best practices to rely on,

00:24:03.370 --> 00:24:06.542
there'll be even more design
patterns that we can start from

00:24:06.542 --> 00:24:09.410
when we wanna design a new thing.

00:24:09.410 --> 00:24:12.990
I don't think we're gonna
go down to the point

00:24:12.990 --> 00:24:15.840
with an intelligence of quotient of 100

00:24:15.840 --> 00:24:17.940
which is average intelligence.

00:24:17.940 --> 00:24:18.773
I don't think so.

00:24:18.773 --> 00:24:21.610
I don't think average people
can do a good user experience.

00:24:21.610 --> 00:24:25.940
It is inherently very
complicated, very demanding

00:24:25.940 --> 00:24:27.510
of brain power because you

00:24:27.510 --> 00:24:30.700
have some enormously
many design dimensions.

00:24:30.700 --> 00:24:31.710
It's just this one thing,

00:24:31.710 --> 00:24:34.310
bigger or smaller buttons
that would not be so hard.

00:24:34.310 --> 00:24:37.010
But there's 50 other things
you gotta consider as well

00:24:37.010 --> 00:24:38.580
and put together and design.

00:24:38.580 --> 00:24:40.950
And that's very, very
complicated to keep all

00:24:40.950 --> 00:24:43.900
of those different things in
your brain at the same time.

00:24:43.900 --> 00:24:47.120
And also we have to do trade-offs

00:24:47.120 --> 00:24:49.640
between very vague criteria, conflict

00:24:49.640 --> 00:24:53.380
and criteria that are usually
not very cleanly specified.

00:24:53.380 --> 00:24:54.290
So because of that,

00:24:54.290 --> 00:24:58.450
it is inherently rather
difficult to do user experience.

00:24:58.450 --> 00:25:00.480
It's not gonna be something
for the average person

00:25:00.480 --> 00:25:01.860
off the street who can do this.

00:25:01.860 --> 00:25:02.693
But I do think

00:25:02.693 --> 00:25:05.930
that we don't need to be
geniuses anymore already now

00:25:05.930 --> 00:25:07.800
and in the future even less so.

00:25:07.800 --> 00:25:09.810
And that's lucky because
I do have this prediction

00:25:09.810 --> 00:25:13.260
that there will be enormously
many more UX professionals

00:25:13.260 --> 00:25:14.380
in the world in the future.

00:25:14.380 --> 00:25:18.210
So right now it's about 1.5 million of us.

00:25:18.210 --> 00:25:20.220
And that means that we are consuming,

00:25:20.220 --> 00:25:23.580
we are accounting for a third of a percent

00:25:23.580 --> 00:25:26.300
of the world's workforce
that has this high level

00:25:26.300 --> 00:25:27.580
of intelligence.

00:25:27.580 --> 00:25:29.550
And that's not so bad.

00:25:29.550 --> 00:25:31.120
A third of a percent is not so bad.

00:25:31.120 --> 00:25:34.260
And I think it is a little bit
hard to hire good UX people

00:25:34.260 --> 00:25:37.750
but it's not because of lack of talent,

00:25:37.750 --> 00:25:39.660
it's because of lack of training.

00:25:39.660 --> 00:25:40.900
So that's...

00:25:40.900 --> 00:25:43.820
We can deal with this. 
But go into the future,

00:25:43.820 --> 00:25:47.710
I think we will have 100
million UX people in the world.

00:25:47.710 --> 00:25:50.740
And now we're talking about
we're gonna consume 11%

00:25:50.740 --> 00:25:52.200
of the world's smart people.

00:25:52.200 --> 00:25:53.370
Oh, workforce,

00:25:53.370 --> 00:25:56.710
not retired people but
people in the workforce 11%.

00:25:56.710 --> 00:26:00.870
And that's a high, high percentage
for just one profession.

00:26:00.870 --> 00:26:03.520
I actually still think
that's probably realistic

00:26:03.520 --> 00:26:06.000
because I think there're
a lot of other professions

00:26:06.000 --> 00:26:09.010
that now account for a large number

00:26:09.010 --> 00:26:11.580
of highly intelligent workforce

00:26:11.580 --> 00:26:14.400
that will require fewer
people in the future

00:26:14.400 --> 00:26:16.450
because artificial
intelligence and computers

00:26:16.450 --> 00:26:20.380
and so forth will take over
a lot of that knowledge work

00:26:20.380 --> 00:26:22.730
and make people much, much more efficient

00:26:22.730 --> 00:26:24.350
in many, many disciplines.

00:26:24.350 --> 00:26:26.150
And that means that there'll be need

00:26:26.150 --> 00:26:28.870
for fewer of these other professions.

00:26:28.870 --> 00:26:30.480
But that doesn't mean
there'll be unemployment,

00:26:30.480 --> 00:26:32.510
that means that those
people will be available

00:26:32.510 --> 00:26:34.620
to have our type of job.

00:26:34.620 --> 00:26:37.100
And so that's how I think
that's gonna play out is

00:26:37.100 --> 00:26:40.203
that there'll be a rebalancing
of the types of jobs

00:26:40.203 --> 00:26:41.254
that people do

00:26:41.254 --> 00:26:44.700
where more of them we'll
be doing user experience

00:26:44.700 --> 00:26:45.880
and few of them...

00:26:45.880 --> 00:26:49.580
I mean still 89% but few
of them will be doing all

00:26:49.580 --> 00:26:54.580
the other types of jobs that
are currently the high IQ jobs.

00:26:54.600 --> 00:26:56.240
In terms of what do we get out of this,

00:26:56.240 --> 00:26:57.640
a lot of people are working on UX.

00:26:57.640 --> 00:26:58.900
So what do we get in return?

00:26:58.900 --> 00:27:02.840
So this chart here shows you
how much a design has improved

00:27:02.840 --> 00:27:05.460
into various measurable metrics

00:27:05.460 --> 00:27:08.410
after you've applied good
UX practices to the design.

00:27:08.410 --> 00:27:10.200
And we've studied these a few times.

00:27:10.200 --> 00:27:13.150
So the first two bars are
actually data from our studies

00:27:13.150 --> 00:27:15.620
and the last one is my
prediction for the future.

00:27:15.620 --> 00:27:17.400
But the first time we started it,

00:27:17.400 --> 00:27:20.020
we found that the signs measurable,

00:27:20.020 --> 00:27:24.330
no metrics improved by 247%

00:27:24.330 --> 00:27:26.510
which is huge improvement, right?

00:27:26.510 --> 00:27:28.010
That's enormous improvement

00:27:28.010 --> 00:27:31.030
in design quality based
on user experience.

00:27:31.030 --> 00:27:34.420
And, now last year we
did the same study again

00:27:34.420 --> 00:27:38.690
and now it's 75% improvement
from doing a good UX.

00:27:38.690 --> 00:27:41.260
Why is it that this
improvement is so much lower?

00:27:41.260 --> 00:27:46.690
It's because back then the
designs were really so bad.

00:27:46.690 --> 00:27:48.840
I mean, honestly, if you
remember there was a lot

00:27:48.840 --> 00:27:52.360
of terrible websites out there
and other products as well.

00:27:52.360 --> 00:27:55.960
And so when you start
from a very low baseline,

00:27:55.960 --> 00:27:57.830
you can improve it alot.

00:27:57.830 --> 00:28:00.810
Nowadays, certainly the
designs are still not perfect

00:28:00.810 --> 00:28:04.630
but user interfaces are much
better than they used to be.

00:28:04.630 --> 00:28:08.780
And so this means that if we
apply our good best practices

00:28:08.780 --> 00:28:10.420
from starting from a higher point,

00:28:10.420 --> 00:28:13.630
we can not improve them by quite as much.

00:28:13.630 --> 00:28:17.900
So, but 75% is still a
good improvement in metrics

00:28:17.900 --> 00:28:19.640
from doing UX.

00:28:19.640 --> 00:28:22.780
Still, I think this is an
example of misallocation

00:28:22.780 --> 00:28:25.950
of resources that we don't
do even more of this.

00:28:25.950 --> 00:28:28.740
Because we did another study to find out

00:28:28.740 --> 00:28:32.090
how many people on an average product team

00:28:32.090 --> 00:28:35.000
had two UX professionals
or designers or researchers

00:28:35.000 --> 00:28:37.390
and that's an average 11%.

00:28:37.390 --> 00:28:41.360
Now, sometimes more, sometimes
less but on average 11%.

00:28:41.360 --> 00:28:47.600
Now, if 11% of the team
improve the product by 75%,

00:28:47.600 --> 00:28:50.060
wow, you should do more of that, right?

00:28:50.060 --> 00:28:51.890
And so that's what I'm
predicting in the future.

00:28:51.890 --> 00:28:53.810
And right now I think it's a misallocation

00:28:53.810 --> 00:28:56.570
of resources that we don't do even more UX

00:28:56.570 --> 00:28:59.140
but it takes a while to grow this.

00:28:59.140 --> 00:29:01.280
So my prediction is we're gonna grow more

00:29:01.280 --> 00:29:04.860
and more UX people but at
the same time picking all

00:29:04.860 --> 00:29:07.313
the low-hanging fruit
and design getting better

00:29:07.313 --> 00:29:08.570
and better and better.

00:29:08.570 --> 00:29:10.810
And so the improvement will get lower

00:29:10.810 --> 00:29:11.910
and lower unfortunately.

00:29:11.910 --> 00:29:15.987
But, honestly, so that make us feel like,

00:29:15.987 --> 00:29:18.000
"Well, UX is getting
less and less important"

00:29:18.000 --> 00:29:19.610
but actually not so.

00:29:19.610 --> 00:29:22.920
Because what happens is
that those 25% improvement

00:29:22.920 --> 00:29:26.370
will be applying to more
and more things and more

00:29:26.370 --> 00:29:28.370
and more important things as well.

00:29:28.370 --> 00:29:32.830
So right now my estimate
is that UX, our profession

00:29:32.830 --> 00:29:35.900
our field contributes
$0.5 trillion per year

00:29:35.900 --> 00:29:37.600
to the world economy.

00:29:37.600 --> 00:29:40.240
And by 2050, I'm
predicting that we will be

00:29:40.240 --> 00:29:43.990
contributing $10 trillion a
year to the world economy.

00:29:43.990 --> 00:29:49.180
So vastly more each thing
we do will have less impact

00:29:49.180 --> 00:29:51.010
but then we'll do so many more things

00:29:51.010 --> 00:29:52.190
that the total impact,

00:29:52.190 --> 00:29:55.980
which is what matters,
will be much bigger.

00:29:55.980 --> 00:29:59.500
Now I wanna end by telling you
the story of the Dodo bird.

00:29:59.500 --> 00:30:02.970
So the Dodo is mainly
famous for being extinct,

00:30:02.970 --> 00:30:05.110
sad to say for the poor bird.

00:30:05.110 --> 00:30:07.350
But it used to live on the Island

00:30:07.350 --> 00:30:08.940
of Mauritius in the Indian ocean.

00:30:08.940 --> 00:30:12.450
And before it became extinct,
luckily there were a few ships

00:30:12.450 --> 00:30:15.020
that landed on this island and
caught a few of these birds

00:30:15.020 --> 00:30:18.300
and took them back to museums
and so they got stuffed.

00:30:18.300 --> 00:30:20.950
So you can see in the Natural
History Museum in London,

00:30:20.950 --> 00:30:22.550
you could see the photograph
I took on the left.

00:30:22.550 --> 00:30:24.490
That's a bird that's stuffed
and you can see it today.

00:30:24.490 --> 00:30:28.990
You can see how a Dodo
used to look in the museum.

00:30:28.990 --> 00:30:31.480
Now, these sailors who was
sailing around the Indian ocean

00:30:31.480 --> 00:30:34.460
they also pass by another
Island in the Indian ocean

00:30:34.460 --> 00:30:36.520
called Reunion Island.

00:30:36.520 --> 00:30:38.320
And they didn't land on this island

00:30:38.320 --> 00:30:40.570
but they looked at it with their telescope

00:30:40.570 --> 00:30:42.350
and they saw some big birds there.

00:30:42.350 --> 00:30:44.280
And they saw they looked
a lot like the Dodo bird

00:30:44.280 --> 00:30:45.630
that they just caught in Mauritius.

00:30:45.630 --> 00:30:47.880
So they went back to London
and talked to the guys

00:30:47.880 --> 00:30:50.937
at the museum and said, "We
saw a Dodo bird at Reunion

00:30:50.937 --> 00:30:53.690
"but we don't get to get to
catch one for you, sorry."

00:30:53.690 --> 00:30:56.067
And so the museum people
said, "Well, we wanna

00:30:56.067 --> 00:31:00.010
"have that Reunion Island
Dodo as well in exhibit."

00:31:00.010 --> 00:31:01.100
But they didn't have one.

00:31:01.100 --> 00:31:03.160
So they just make up a fake bird.

00:31:03.160 --> 00:31:04.970
They made up a bird and stuffed

00:31:04.970 --> 00:31:06.650
and put that on display as well

00:31:06.650 --> 00:31:09.950
with a little sign saying,
"Here's the Reunion Island Dodo."

00:31:09.950 --> 00:31:13.070
Now it turns out that there
is no Dodo on Reunion Island,

00:31:13.070 --> 00:31:15.690
there never was a Dodo on Reunion Island.

00:31:15.690 --> 00:31:17.340
So those sailors who spotted the bird

00:31:17.340 --> 00:31:21.510
in their telescope must have
seen some other type of bird.

00:31:21.510 --> 00:31:22.990
Now, I just had to say to the credit of

00:31:22.990 --> 00:31:25.000
the Natural History Museum in London,

00:31:25.000 --> 00:31:28.650
they have now clean about
they made up a fake bird

00:31:28.650 --> 00:31:30.607
and they have another
sign on it that says,

00:31:30.607 --> 00:31:31.657
"There was never this bird

00:31:31.657 --> 00:31:33.390
"but we made it up for this reason."

00:31:33.390 --> 00:31:35.950
So you can still see that fake bird today

00:31:35.950 --> 00:31:39.690
as a kind of lesson in the
downsides of making up things.

00:31:39.690 --> 00:31:42.130
And I think there should
be a lesson to not just

00:31:42.130 --> 00:31:44.560
to the Natural History
Museum but also to us.

00:31:44.560 --> 00:31:48.740
Because UX unfortunately
has a lot of fake birds.

00:31:48.740 --> 00:31:52.650
We have a lot of cases where
we make up stories about users

00:31:52.650 --> 00:31:54.077
and say, "Well, people
are kinda used to this,"

00:31:54.077 --> 00:31:56.420
"so they kinda like
that or they want this."

00:31:56.420 --> 00:31:59.490
And we don't know because we
didn't study actual users.

00:31:59.490 --> 00:32:01.750
And that's what I would call a fake bird.

00:32:01.750 --> 00:32:03.250
And we have a lot of those

00:32:03.250 --> 00:32:05.160
unfortunately in our field as well.

00:32:05.160 --> 00:32:08.800
And I'm sad to say, I'll
predict we'll keep having those.

00:32:08.800 --> 00:32:10.590
Because the history of user experience is

00:32:10.590 --> 00:32:13.960
that every time we get a new technology

00:32:13.960 --> 00:32:16.240
or some new opportunity for design,

00:32:16.240 --> 00:32:20.380
it's like a two-step forward
one-step back scenario

00:32:20.380 --> 00:32:22.470
which of course net is an improvement.

00:32:22.470 --> 00:32:25.910
But honestly, every time we
get something new we can do,

00:32:25.910 --> 00:32:28.300
some people make up
fake stories about users

00:32:28.300 --> 00:32:32.600
and then they do bad design
and we have to fix all of that

00:32:32.600 --> 00:32:33.820
and that's a sad thing.

00:32:33.820 --> 00:32:34.800
And I really just predict

00:32:34.800 --> 00:32:37.230
that that's unfortunately gonna continue.

00:32:37.230 --> 00:32:40.220
I think that's an unfortunate scenario.

00:32:40.220 --> 00:32:43.680
But that said, remember
that by 2050 I'm predicting

00:32:43.680 --> 00:32:46.790
we will have 100 million
UX people in the world.

00:32:46.790 --> 00:32:47.660
And so this means

00:32:47.660 --> 00:32:51.830
that they will be an extremely
large number of new insights,

00:32:51.830 --> 00:32:54.540
new user insights, new design ideas,

00:32:54.540 --> 00:32:56.870
various improvement in the
user interface coming out

00:32:56.870 --> 00:32:59.457
and some fake birds, fake data,

00:32:59.457 --> 00:33:02.550
bad design coming out as well, yes.

00:33:02.550 --> 00:33:04.950
But also a great number of good ones.

00:33:04.950 --> 00:33:07.763
And now user experience
we're actually lucky

00:33:07.763 --> 00:33:10.950
that we have designed Darwinism,

00:33:10.950 --> 00:33:12.500
the survival of the fittest

00:33:12.500 --> 00:33:15.140
or the survival of the high most usability

00:33:15.140 --> 00:33:18.540
which is that users
will go where it's easy,

00:33:18.540 --> 00:33:21.390
where they're treated well,
where things are nice for them.

00:33:21.390 --> 00:33:22.880
If they get a bad design,

00:33:22.880 --> 00:33:25.170
they'll use it less and
they'll stop using it

00:33:25.170 --> 00:33:27.570
or they will not go to that
website more than once.

00:33:27.570 --> 00:33:30.600
If it's a good design, they'll
come back and use it more.

00:33:30.600 --> 00:33:34.630
And so the design Darwinism
is that gradually,

00:33:34.630 --> 00:33:36.680
the good user experience actually wins.

00:33:36.680 --> 00:33:41.010
But there is temporarily a
lot of bad mutations as well

00:33:41.010 --> 00:33:42.410
and we have to suffer under...

00:33:42.410 --> 00:33:45.460
But designed Darwinism means
that eventually we get better.

00:33:45.460 --> 00:33:48.880
That's why that prediction
of having so much innovation

00:33:48.880 --> 00:33:51.970
in the world is I think
is extremely positive

00:33:51.970 --> 00:33:54.940
because that means that
so many, many, many

00:33:54.940 --> 00:33:56.300
many ideas and so forth

00:33:56.300 --> 00:33:59.520
which we can call kind of the best ones.

00:33:59.520 --> 00:34:02.330
Now, because of all of these changes,

00:34:02.330 --> 00:34:06.050
my ultimate prediction
about user experience is

00:34:06.050 --> 00:34:09.000
that we are gonna be the digital mermaid.

00:34:09.000 --> 00:34:11.120
We're gonna be like the little mermaid.

00:34:11.120 --> 00:34:12.290
When I say the "Little Mermaid"

00:34:12.290 --> 00:34:15.800
I mean the original fairytale
by Hans Christian Andersen,

00:34:15.800 --> 00:34:17.520
I don't mean the kind of prettified movie

00:34:17.520 --> 00:34:18.900
that was made later.

00:34:18.900 --> 00:34:21.850
But in the original fairytale
about the little mermaid,

00:34:21.850 --> 00:34:25.630
the ending is actually
kind of tragic because

00:34:25.630 --> 00:34:29.280
or at least bittersweet
because yes he gets the prince

00:34:29.280 --> 00:34:31.300
but at a very high cost,

00:34:31.300 --> 00:34:34.330
at the cost of everything
that made her special.

00:34:34.330 --> 00:34:37.430
She has to become human and she loses...

00:34:37.430 --> 00:34:38.890
She can't swim with the fishes

00:34:38.890 --> 00:34:41.860
and all the various things
that used to make her special.

00:34:41.860 --> 00:34:43.710
She has lost all of that.

00:34:43.710 --> 00:34:46.940
And I think this is what's
gonna happen to us as well.

00:34:46.940 --> 00:34:50.810
We will do what used to make us special.

00:34:50.810 --> 00:34:53.550
We used to be special because
we were a very small elite,

00:34:53.550 --> 00:34:56.260
very few people who
knew how to make designs

00:34:56.260 --> 00:34:57.470
and websites work.

00:34:57.470 --> 00:35:00.130
Most people didn't and
made terrible products.

00:35:00.130 --> 00:35:02.470
We were the few people that
knew how to make it better.

00:35:02.470 --> 00:35:03.880
Whenever we worked on something,

00:35:03.880 --> 00:35:06.040
man, several hundred
percents improvements.

00:35:06.040 --> 00:35:07.440
It was like, wow, wow, wow

00:35:07.440 --> 00:35:09.290
here comes the UX and we are the heroes

00:35:09.290 --> 00:35:12.300
and we will just make
things so much better.

00:35:12.300 --> 00:35:14.360
Well, as I said my prediction is

00:35:14.360 --> 00:35:16.570
we will make things only a
little better in the future

00:35:16.570 --> 00:35:19.280
because it's gonna get already much better

00:35:19.280 --> 00:35:20.820
and navigate so many innovations

00:35:20.820 --> 00:35:22.770
that each individual thing we work on

00:35:22.770 --> 00:35:24.110
is not gonna be so much.

00:35:24.110 --> 00:35:28.150
We're not gonna be special, 
it's gonna be a 100 million of us.

00:35:28.150 --> 00:35:30.780
So, is that good or bad?

00:35:30.780 --> 00:35:34.370
I think ultimately it's good
because there is that prize.

00:35:34.370 --> 00:35:36.300
The little mermaid she won the prince,

00:35:36.300 --> 00:35:38.100
we were gonna win the world.

00:35:38.100 --> 00:35:42.380
We're gonna make the
world so much better for

